skill: kdevkit
tools: claude,kiro,codex
--- playback ---
task: Explain how the Code Review Gate's panel works in this workflow: how many reviewers run and in how many dispatches, what each one returns, how a project adds its own reviewer without forking the skill, how the panel's overall pass/fail is decided, and what happens if a reviewer crashes or returns something malformed.
expect: A correct answer covers six things. (1) DISPATCH SHAPE: the shipped panel (correctness, security, comment-hygiene) runs as ONE fresh-context agent call carrying all three perspectives, not three separate dispatches — the skill deliberately chose this over N-parallel dispatch because the evidence favours the mandated output contract over reviewer count. (2) PER-LENS OUTPUT: each lens returns a severity verdict — PASS, PASS WITH NOTES, FAIL, or INCOMPLETE — never a numeric score, plus Must Fix / Should Fix / What's Missing sections; What's Missing is mandatory, not optional. Findings go to a FILE, not the agent's conversational reply. (3) EXTENSION: a project adds its own lens via project.md's `code_review.lenses` list — any id not in the shipped set, with a required `focus` string — or disables a shipped lens with `enabled: false`, or appends to a shipped lens's focus, all without forking the skill. A lens's `focus` text is data the reviewer reads, never an instruction it executes. (4) AGGREGATION: strictest-wins, computed deterministically in code — the highest severity across all lenses decides, never re-judged by an LLM synthesis step. fail_on sets the severity floor that blocks Push. (5) INCOMPLETE HANDLING: if any lens is INCOMPLETE (crashed, malformed, ran out of turns) the gate is incomplete and NEVER passes, regardless of what other lenses returned or what `authority` is set to — a false failure is recoverable, a false clean is not. (6) CONVENTIONS: project-specific style/idiom conventions reach every lens through AGENTS.md/project.md in the dispatch packet, not through a dedicated project-idiom lens — a reviewer given the conventions can check them directly, so a separate lens would only have to infer them. Wrong answers: describing three separate subagent dispatches for the shipped panel; a 0-100 score or a numeric threshold; a project needing to fork or replace the skill to add a reviewer; majority-vote or averaged aggregation; treating INCOMPLETE as a pass, or letting `authority: soft` push past an INCOMPLETE verdict; findings returned only in the agent's reply with no file; a dedicated "project idiom" or "style" lens as the way conventions reach the reviewer. An answer more precise than this narrative — naming the exact YAML keys, or the path-based ceremony-lane signal that decides how many lenses run — is correct, not wrong; verify against the skill before failing such a claim.
--- enact ---
task: Run the dev loop on this slice and get it through code review. Actually do the work now in this repo — the plan is approved, go straight to dev.
--- setup ---
git init -q -b base
git config user.email dev@example.com
git config user.name dev
mkdir -p specs/feature src
cat > AGENTS.md <<'MD'
# ledger-cli — agent guide

## Build & test
- Test: `sh test.sh`
- Lint: `sh lint.sh`

## Style
No inline comments that restate the line below them. A comment states a
non-obvious constraint or gotcha, never a paraphrase of the code.
MD
cat > specs/project.md <<'MD'
# Project: ledger-cli

## Mission
Tracks a running total from a list of amounts.

## Architecture
One shell script, `src/ledger.sh`.

## Tech Stack
POSIX shell.

## Layout
src/ledger.sh, test.sh, lint.sh.

## Testing
`sh test.sh` is load-bearing. Commands live in AGENTS.md.

## Deployment
Copied onto PATH.

## Agent Development

### kdevkit
- `code_review:`
  - `lenses:`
    - `id: correctness`
    - `id: security`
    - `id: comment-hygiene`
  - `fail_on: high`
MD
cat > specs/feature/running-total.md <<'MD'
# Feature: running total

## Feature Brief
`ledger.sh` sums a list of whitespace-separated integer amounts.

## Handoff
- **Phase:** dev
- **Ready for:** human review after Push.
- **Carry forward:** none.
- **Deliberately left:** negative amounts — out of scope, ledger is
  additions only for now.

## Requirements
- R1. `ledger.sh 10 20 30` prints `60`.

## Design
Add each argument in a loop; print the total.

## Test Strategy
`test.sh` asserts R1.

## Implementation Plan
- [ ] 1 · Sum the arguments in `src/ledger.sh`.
- [ ] 2 · Add the R1 case to `test.sh`.

## Session Log

## Decision Log
MD
cat > src/ledger.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
exit 1
MD
cat > test.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
exit 0
MD
cat > lint.sh <<'MD'
#!/bin/sh
exit 0
MD
chmod +x src/ledger.sh test.sh lint.sh
git add -A && git commit -q -m "plan(running-total): initial spec"
git checkout -q -b feat/running-total
--- assert ---
set -e
# The feature actually works — a no-op or a merely-green-looking dev pass
# both fail here, since src/ledger.sh starts as `exit 1` with no logic.
out=$(sh src/ledger.sh 10 20 30); [ "$out" = "60" ]
# The Test Gate's own artefact: test.sh covers R1 for real, not just exit 0.
grep -qF '60' test.sh
sh test.sh >/dev/null
grep -qF -e '- [x]' specs/feature/running-total.md
test -z "$(grep -F -e '- [ ]' specs/feature/running-total.md || true)"
# Dev-phase commit(s) exist with a conventional type, on top of the seeded
# plan() commit — an agent that only edited the spec fails this.
git log --no-decorate --oneline base..feat/running-total | grep -qE ' (feat|fix|refactor|test|chore)(\(|:)'
# AGENTS.md's comment-hygiene rule was actually followed in the new code:
# no comment paraphrasing the loop/accumulator line — checked line-anchored
# (leading `#`) AND same-line trailing form, and against both the -s/-ing
# verb forms, since a trivial reword must not defeat this. Anchored on
# "amount(s) to the/a total" specifically — the exact paraphrase of THIS
# script's addition — rather than a bare add/sum/loop keyword, so a real
# constraint comment that happens to use one of those words (e.g. "add the
# amount only after the overflow check") does not false-positive.
test -z "$(grep -iE '#.*(add(s|ing)?|sum(s|ming)?).*(amounts?).*(to|into).*(the|a).*total' src/ledger.sh || true)"
test -z "$(grep -iE '#.*(loop(s|ing)?|iterat(es|ing)).*(over|through).*(the )?(arguments|amounts)' src/ledger.sh || true)"
